    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The historic core centers on the 1807 Quaker Meetinghouse, a 1850 shoe factory, and a 1830s store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livingplaces.com/NY/Schenectady_County/Duanesburg_Town/Quaker_Street_Historic_District.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Italianate homes dominate the streetscape, with occasional Queen Anne and Eastlake touches.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livingplaces.com/NY/Schenectady_County/Duanesburg_Town/Quaker_Street_Historic_District.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Route 7 and Gallupville Road, two of the oldest thoroughfares, host early Federal style buildings and the only remaining historic non‑residential structures.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livingplaces.com/NY/Schenectady_County/Duanesburg_Town/Quaker_Street_Historic_District.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The hamlet grew around a Quaker (Society of Friends) settlement that shaped Duanesburg’s early development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livingplaces.com/NY/Schenectady_County/Duanesburg_Town/Quaker_Street_Historic_District.html"
    },
    {
